<Layouts.public page_title={@page_title}>
  <.updated updated_on={@updated_on} />

  <p class="legal-lede">
    {@service_name} is a companion portal for a firstmate crew: it shows Archify
    diagrams, pull requests, issues, work queues and no-mistakes runs in one
    place, stores each tenant's integration credentials, and relays Discord
    commands to the crew. These terms are the agreement between you and {@operator}, who runs this instance.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Using the service</h2>
  <p>
    Access is by invitation. Sign-in is restricted to accounts your operator has
    allowed — through an OIDC identity provider or a password-based local
    bootstrap account. An email-domain allowlist is an optional extra restriction
    on OIDC sign-in. There is no open registration and no anonymous use beyond
    these public pages.
  </p>
  <p>
    You are responsible for what happens under your account. Session cookies,
    the JSON Web Token <code>fm-steer</code> stores after a device-code sign-in,
    and any agent service token you are issued are all credentials: keep them to
    yourself, and tell your operator promptly if one is exposed.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Acceptable use</h2>
  <p>You agree not to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      probe, scan or attack the service, or try to reach another tenant's data —
      tenancy is enforced by the API, and working around it is not research
      unless your operator asked you for it in writing;
    </li>
    <li>
      work around rate limits, sign-in lockouts or the allowlist, or automate
      sign-in attempts;
    </li>
    <li>
      upload content that is unlawful, that infringes someone else's rights, or
      that you have no right to place on your operator's infrastructure;
    </li>
    <li>
      use the Discord integration to send unsolicited messages, or to act in a
      guild without that guild's permission.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Your content</h2>
  <p>
    Diagrams, pull request and issue records, queue messages, run history and
    everything else you or your agents submit stay yours. You grant {@operator} permission to store, process and display that content for the purpose of
    running the service for you — including keeping change history, since the
    portal versions records so a crew can see what changed and when.
  </p>
  <p>
    Content you publish through a diagram share link is readable by anyone who
    has the link, and by the link-preview crawlers of the chat services you paste
    it into. Treat a share link as public.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Integration credentials</h2>
  <p>
    Tenants store their own credentials in the portal — Discord keys, GitHub
    tokens, provider API keys. Those values are encrypted before they reach the
    database and are never returned to you in plaintext over HTTP or MCP; once
    stored, a secret can be replaced but not read back.
  </p>
  <p>
    Scoping those credentials is your job. Issue the narrowest token that does
    what you need, rotate it on your own schedule, and revoke it at its source if
    you suspect exposure. Neither the portal nor {@operator} can revoke a third
    party's token on your behalf.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Discord</h2>
  <p>
    Where a tenant connects Discord, the portal receives interaction payloads
    that Discord sends to its interactions endpoint, verifies their signature,
    and hands the command to that tenant's crew. Installing the application in a
    guild requires the guild's authority, and everyone in that guild remains
    subject to Discord's own terms and policies as well as these.
  </p>
  <p>
    Commands issued in a guild are visible to the crew that serves it. Do not put
    anything in a Discord command that you would not put in the portal.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Availability</h2>
  <p>
    The service is provided as-is, with no uptime commitment and no warranty of
    any kind. It is operated for a working crew, not sold as a product, and it
    can be restarted, upgraded or taken down without notice. Keep your own copy
    of anything you cannot afford to lose.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Suspension and termination</h2>
  <p>
    An account may be suspended or removed by {@operator} for breaking these
    terms, endangering the service or its other users, or where the law requires
    it. You may stop using the service at any time and ask your operator to
    delete your account; see the <.link href={~p"/privacy"}>Privacy Policy</.link>
    for what that removes.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Liability</h2>
  <p>
    To the fullest extent the law allows, {@operator} is not liable for lost
    profits, lost data, or indirect or consequential loss arising from your use
    of the service. Nothing here limits liability that cannot lawfully be
    limited.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Changes</h2>
  <p>
    These terms change when the service does. The date at the top of this page is
    the date the text last changed; continuing to use the service after a change
    means you accept the current version.
  </p>

  <h2 :if={@governing_law}>10. Governing law</h2>
  <p :if={@governing_law}>
    These terms are governed by the law of {@governing_law}.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <.contact email={@contact_email} operator={@operator} />
</Layouts.public>
